

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mSection 2 — Data model and persistence[0m

  Three records, all in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m under versioned keys ([38;5;153mtasks/v1[39m, [38;5;153mnotifications/v1[39m, [38;5;153msettings/v1[39m), each stored as [38;5;153m{ [39m
  [38;5;153mversion, data }[39m so a later migration has a place to branch.

  [1mTask[0m

  { id, title, [36mdueAt[39m: [36mnumber[39m | [34mnull[39m, [36mcompletedAt[39m: [36mnumber[39m | [34mnull[39m, [36mcreatedAt[39m: [36mnumber[39m }

  [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is epoch milliseconds — an absolute instant. [3mAssumption: a reminder should fire at the same absolute moment [0m
  [3mregardless of timezone changes; validate by confirming you don't expect "Friday 5pm" to follow you across timezones.[0m
  Storing wall-clock-plus-zone instead is possible but meaningfully more complex, and I don't think it's worth it for a
  local single-user app.

  [1mNotificationRecord[0m

  { id, taskId, [36mkind[39m: [31m'due-soon'[39m | [31m'due'[39m | [31m'overdue'[39m, [36mfiredAt[39m: [36mnumber[39m, [36mreadAt[39m: [36mnumber[39m | [34mnull[39m }

  [1mSettings[0m — just [38;5;153m{ leadTimeMinutes: number }[39m, defaulting to 15.

  Two rules that matter more than the shapes:

  [1mDedup.[0m A task fires each [38;5;153mkind[39m at most once. The engine's [38;5;153malreadyFired[39m input is the set of [38;5;153mtaskId:kind[39m pairs derived
  from existing notification records, which is what stops a reminder re-firing on every tick and every page load.

  [1mInvalidation on edit.[0m If a task's [38;5;153mdueAt[39m changes, its existing notification records are discarded so the new date can
  fire cleanly — otherwise rescheduling a task to next week leaves it permanently unable to remind you. Completing a
  task does the same for any of its not-yet-fired kinds. This is the rule most likely to be missed if it isn't written
  down, so it goes in the spec explicitly.

  Does the model look right, and is the absolute-instant assumption on [38;5;153mdueAt[39m correct for your use?

